.\" fmevent(1)
.Dd October 5, 2026
.Dt FMEVENT 1
.Os macOS 27
.Sh NAME
.Nm fmevent
.Nd a semantic if command line tool for macOS
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op options
.Ar condition
.Nm
.Op options
.Ar condition
.Ar file ...
.Nm
.Fl -route
.Ar routes.json
.Op options
.Nm
.Fl -schema
.Ar schema.json
.Ar instruction
.Nm
.Cm doctor
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a semantic
.Dq if
command line tool for macOS.
It turns a plain-English question about stdin into an exit status, so shells
branch on meaning rather than on text.
It runs Apple's on-device model.
Nothing leaves your Mac, and the model only ever classifies.
You decide what happens next.
.Pp
.Nm
evaluates a natural-language condition against its input using Apple's on-device
Foundation Model, by way of the
.Xr fm 1
command shipped with macOS 27, and reports the answer as an exit status.
.Pp
The model is asked one question: what does this input mean.
Every decision about what happens next \(em which route runs, what the status is \(em
is made by
.Nm
itself, deterministically.
.Nm
is not an agent, and never asks the model to produce a command.
.Pp
Input is read from standard input, or from the
.Ar file
operands.
.Sh NOTES
Two mistakes account for most surprising results.
.Pp
Error messages are written to standard error, which does not pass through a
pipe, so
.Nm
classifies an empty stream and says so:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ls dsd | fmevent "command failed"
fmevent: input is empty; nothing to classify
.Ed
.Pp
Redirect it when the thing to be classified is an error message:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ls dsd 2>&1 | fmevent "command failed"
.Ed
.Pp
Second, the model is not deterministic: identical input and condition can yield
different answers across invocations.
Pass
.Fl -greedy
whenever the answer must be reproducible, in particular in CI and in tests.
An answer that changes between runs is not one automation can be built on.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It 0
The condition is satisfied, or a route matched.
.It 1
The condition is not satisfied, or no route matched.
.It 2
.Nm
itself failed.
.El
.Pp
A failure of
.Xr fm 1
is always 2, never 1, so that a broken tool cannot be mistaken for a
negative answer.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl -route Ar file
Read routes from
.Ar file
and dispatch to the matching one.
See
.Sx ROUTES .
.It Fl -schema Ar file
Structured extraction rather than a yes/no decision.
The model's JSON is written to standard output unchanged.
.Ar instruction
describes what to extract.
.It Fl -json
Write a machine-readable result to standard output, as a single JSON object:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va matched
The decision.
It always agrees with the exit status.
.It Va confidence
The model's self-reported certainty, from 0.0 to 1.0.
This is a self-report and not a calibrated probability; see
.Sx CONFIDENCE .
.It Va evidence
The text the model quoted to justify its answer.
Informational only; it must never be treated as authoritative by a program.
.El
.It Fl -explain
Print the model's reasoning.
Informational only; it must never be treated as authoritative by a program.
.It Fl -min-confidence Ar n
Require the model's self-reported confidence to be at least
.Ar n .
Defaults to 0; see
.Sx CONFIDENCE .
.It Fl -strict
Refuse, with status 2, when the input looks like a prompt injection attempt,
and require the evidence the model quotes to appear in the input.
Fails closed.
Not valid together with
.Fl -on-error
values other than
.Cm fail .
.It Fl -dry-run
In route mode, print the decision and execute nothing.
.It Fl -max-input Ar bytes
Budget for the payload.
Defaults to 12288.
Input beyond it is elided in the middle so that the head and tail survive,
and a warning is printed.
.It Fl -head Ar n
Keep only the first
.Ar n
lines.
.It Fl -tail Ar n
Keep only the last
.Ar n
lines.
.It Fl -timeout Ar seconds
Abandon the model after this long.
Defaults to 120.
macOS has no
.Xr timeout 1 ,
so this is implemented with a watchdog.
.It Fl -on-error Ar mode
What to do when the model fails:
.Cm fail
(exit 2, the default),
.Cm true ,
or
.Cm false .
.It Fl -greedy
Use greedy sampling.
The model is otherwise not deterministic; use this in CI and in tests, where
the same input must produce the same answer.
.It Fl -use-case Ar case
.Cm general
or
.Cm content-tagging .
.It Fl -guardrails Ar level
.Cm default
or
.Cm permissive-content-transformations .
.It Fl q , -quiet
Suppress diagnostics on standard error.
.It Fl h , -help
Print usage.
.It Fl V , -version
Print the version.
.El
.Sh ROUTES
A routes file is JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "settings": { "min_confidence": 0.0 },
  "route": [
    { "name": "security",
      "when": "a security vulnerability or credential is involved",
      "priority": 50,
      "run": ["./handle-security.sh"] },
    { "name": "dependency",
      "when": "an unresolved or conflicting dependency",
      "priority": 20,
      "run": ["./handle-dependency.sh"] },
    { "name": "dependency-security",
      "when": "a dependency problem involving a security vulnerability",
      "all_of": ["dependency", "security"],
      "priority": 100,
      "run": ["./handle-dependency-security.sh"] }
  ],
  "fallback": { "name": "unknown", "run": ["./handle-unknown.sh"] }
}
.Ed
.Pp
The model reports every route whose
.Va when
condition the input satisfies.
Names it invents are discarded.
The survivors are ranked by
.Va priority ,
highest first, ties going to the earlier declaration, and the winner's
.Va run
array is executed directly, without a shell, so nothing in the configuration
can inject a command.
A leading
.Pa ./
or
.Pa ../
is resolved against the directory holding the routes file, so the file is
self-contained and may be invoked from any working directory; anything else is
looked up on
.Ev PATH
as usual.
.Pp
.Va all_of
composes routes deterministically.
A model recognises the parts of a compound situation but reliably declines to
report the whole, so a composite route is derived by the engine whenever all of
the named routes matched, rather than being asked of the model.
.Pp
In route mode the exit status is that of the executed action; 1 if nothing
matched and no
.Va fallback
was declared; 2 if
.Nm
failed.
.Sh CONFIDENCE
The default threshold is 0 on purpose.
The number the model reports is a self-reported guess with no calibration behind
it, so gating on it by default would produce unpredictable false negatives.
The match/no-match judgement and route abstention carry the decision instead.
Both
.Fl -json
and
.Fl -explain
always surface the number so that a threshold can be chosen against real data.
.Sh SECURITY
.Nm
is not a security boundary.
Text supplied as input can persuade the model to the wrong answer; this was
measured, and neither anti-injection instructions nor delimiter framing of the
payload prevented it.
.Nm
scans input for instruction-like patterns and warns, and
.Fl -strict
turns that into a refusal, but anyone who controls the classified text can
influence the result.
Use
.Nm
to sort, triage, label, and route, not to authorise.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ev FMEVENT_FM
Path to the
.Xr fm 1
binary.
Defaults to /usr/bin/fm.
.It Ev FMEVENT_JQ
Path to the
.Xr jq 1
binary.
Defaults to /usr/bin/jq.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa ~/.fm/
.Xr fm 1
sessions and configuration.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Decide whether a log shows a dependency failure:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cat output.log | fmevent "this is a dependency resolution failure"
.Ed
.Pp
Gate a deploy on a security-sensitive diff:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
git diff | fmevent "this changes authentication or cryptographic behaviour"
.Ed
.Pp
Route a failing test run without executing anything:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pytest 2>&1 | fmevent --route routes.json --dry-run
.Ed
.Pp
Read only the tail of a large log:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
docker logs api | fmevent --tail 500 "this indicates a database failure"
.Ed
.Pp
Extract structured data from a diff, giving the model the shape to fill in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
git diff | fmevent --schema examples/schema/change.json \e
    "analyse whether this changes security-sensitive behaviour" | jq .
.Ed
.Pp
which prints, for example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "summary": "The code checks if the password matches the stored
              password using a constant-time comparison.",
  "severity": "low",
  "category": "authentication",
  "security_sensitive": true
}
.Ed
.Pp
There is no true/false in this mode, so it is not a predicate: the exit status
is 0 when valid JSON was produced and 2 when it was not.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fm 1 ,
.Xr jq 1 ,
.Xr test 1 ,
.Xr grep 1
.Pp
Full documentation, worked examples and the source:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
https://github.com/noinoiexists/fmevent
.Ed
.Sh AUTHOR
.An Nithik R. <https://github.com/noinoiexists/>
.Sh BUGS
The confidence value is a self-report and not a calibrated probability.
The model's evidence text is informational and must not be treated as
authoritative by a program.
.Pp
The model is not reliably deterministic: identical input and condition can
yield different answers across invocations.
.Fl -greedy
removes the sampling randomness and makes repeated runs agree.
.Pp
The model may be persuaded by text inside the input itself, as described under
.Sx SECURITY .
